# Target C-II — the finite-defect strip contains a macroscopic common-transverse ladder

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8623`  
**Dependencies:** coupled adaptive O–O path through `1.8623`; finite-defect KKT strip; complement train rhombus-incidence theorem; audited stopped-train crossing graph  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_common_transverse_ladder_t18623.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The finite-defect strip theorem gives two consecutive selected outer-to-outer trains

\[
\tau_0,\tau_1
\]

bounding one nonhub path region, with crossing-neighborhood symmetric difference bounded by an absolute constant:

\[
|\Gamma(\tau_0)\triangle\Gamma(\tau_1)|\le21729.
\tag{CL.1}
\]

The same two trains belong to the adaptively selected low-terminal-energy family. The audited train-length/terminal-energy theorem therefore makes each of them macroscopic in the strict-rhombus crossing graph. Combining the two facts forces a linear family of complete trains crossing **both** boundaries.

The conclusion is

\[
\boxed{
|\Gamma(\tau_0)\cap\Gamma(\tau_1)|
>0.6608D-o(D).
}
\tag{CL.2}

Moreover this common family is pairwise noncrossing. Thus the selected strip contains a train-crossing `K_{2,M}` with

\[
M>0.6608D-o(D),
\]

whose two-vertex side is `\{\tau_0,\tau_1\}` and whose macroscopic side is an independent family of through-going transverse trains.

This upgrades the finite-defect strip from an abstract bounded-cusp object to a macroscopic rhombic ladder.

---

## 1. Every selected boundary train crosses more than `0.6608D` distinct trains

For a complete outer-to-outer train `tau`, let `r_tau` be the number of strict rhombi traversed by the stopped train. The audited complement train rhombus-incidence theorem proves

\[
\boxed{
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{e_\tau\over2}-o(1),
}
\tag{CL.3}
\]

where `e_tau` is the exact two-terminal directional energy and `delta=d_out/D`.

In the coupled adaptive path theorem every selected train satisfies

\[
e_\tau\le\lambda(\delta),
\qquad
\lambda(\delta)=0.5857-\delta.
\tag{CL.4}
\]

Hence

\[
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{0.5857-\delta\over2}-o(1)
=0.70715-{\delta\over2}-o(1).
\tag{CL.5}
\]

At `t<=1.8623`, the matched atomic floor gives

\[
\delta<0.09246954+o(1).
\]

The exact certificate checks

\[
0.70715-{0.09246954\over2}>0.6609152,
\]

so, uniformly for the selected family,

\[
\boxed{r_\tau>0.6608D-o(D).}
\tag{CL.6}

Each strict rhombus traversed by `tau` is exactly one crossing with another complete stopped train. The audited train crossing graph is simple: two complete trains cross at most once. Therefore distinct rhombi on `tau` give distinct crossing neighbors, and

\[
\boxed{|Γ(\tau)|=r_\tau>0.6608D-o(D).}
\tag{CL.7}

In particular (CL.7) holds for both strip boundaries `tau_0,tau_1`.

---

## 2. Finite neighborhood change forces a linear common family

Put

\[
A=\Gamma(\tau_0),
\qquad
B=\Gamma(\tau_1).
\]

The finite-defect theorem gives (CL.1). Since

\[
|A\cap B|
\ge\min(|A|,|B|)-|A\triangle B|,
\]

(CL.1) and (CL.7) imply

\[
|A\cap B|
>0.6608D-o(D)-21729.
\]

The fixed constant is `o(D)`, hence

\[
\boxed{
|A\cap B|>0.6608D-o(D).
}
\tag{CL.8}

Call this common family `\mathcal C`.

Every `sigma in mathcal C` is a complete train which crosses `tau_0` once and `tau_1` once, in two distinct strict rhombi. Its midpoint subpath between those crossings lies in the selected strip region and consists entirely of strict-rhombus steps. Thus every member of `mathcal C` is a genuine through-going transverse train for the strip.

---

## 3. The common transverse family is pairwise noncrossing

Take distinct

\[
\sigma,\rho\in\mathcal C.
\]

Both cross `tau_0`. If `sigma` and `rho` also crossed each other, then the three complete trains

\[
\tau_0,\sigma,\rho
\]

would be pairwise crossing.

But the audited stopped-train crossing graph is triangle-free: crossing in a strict rhombus forces projective phase separation strictly greater than `pi/3`, and three directions on the projective circle cannot have all three pairwise separations greater than `pi/3`.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\sigma\text{ and }\rho\text{ do not cross.}
}
\tag{CL.9}

Thus `mathcal C` is a pairwise-noncrossing family.

Since every member crosses each of `tau_0,tau_1`, the selected trains induce a complete bipartite crossing pattern

\[
\boxed{K_{2,M},\qquad M=|\mathcal C|>0.6608D-o(D).}
\tag{CL.10}

The common transverse paths are consistently ordered between the two boundary train crosscuts.

---

## 4. Interaction with the finite-defect strip

The same strip already satisfies

```text
N_i <= 21729 switching actual diameter rows;
B_i < 21730/D switching KKT mass;
||F_{i+1}-F_i|| < 21730;
L_i <= 21729 complete-train terminal occurrences;
<= 4345 bounded exceptional faces;
|Gamma(tau_0) triangle Gamma(tau_1)| <= 21729;
all diameter switching is one-sided toward the common hub;
exact nonzero layer reaction is generated by <=2 actual diameter bars.
```

The present theorem adds

```text
>0.6608 D through-going transverse complete trains;
those transverse trains are pairwise noncrossing;
each crosses both strip boundaries exactly once.
```

Hence almost all rhombi of either boundary train belong to the same ordered transverse ladder. Only finitely many boundary rhombi are not shared by the opposite boundary's crossing neighborhood.

This is the first current `t<=1.8623` theorem reducing the high-`t` branch to a fixed-complexity defect set sitting inside a **macroscopic two-by-linear rhombic train grid**.

The remaining closing question is correspondingly local: exploit the bounded exceptional/diameter set against this ordered `K_{2,Theta(D)}` scaffold to produce either a clean subrectangle admitting a diameter-decreasing multishear or a forbidden convex-diameter endpoint pattern.

No such final deformation is claimed here.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only macroscopic common-transverse ladder through `t<=1.8623`. C-II and Level I remain open.
